# Multi-Site Item Scoping — What Is Shared, What Is Not

Load-bearing for any decision about adding a site to an existing tenant: collection placement,
serialization grain, component reuse, and visual divergence all follow from this table.

| Item type | Scope |
|---|---|
| Renderings | **COLLECTION** |
| Datasource / project templates | **COLLECTION** |
| `Available Renderings` | **SITE** |
| `Headless Variants` | **SITE** |
| Page / Partial Designs, Placeholder Settings, Styles | **SITE** |

## Consequences — do not re-derive these, they follow from the table

1. **A second site in an existing collection reuses the data model by construction.** Same rendering
   items, same datasource templates, zero new templates for reused components.
2. **Author isolation does not require a separate collection.** The site-scoped items above cannot leak
   into another site's Pages UI; the gate is `Available Renderings`, which is per-site. Choosing a new
   collection to buy isolation pays a full clone of the item estate plus a permanent schema-drift tax
   for something the site scope already provides.
3. **Visual divergence is not a Sitecore problem when the new site has its own head app.** A reused
   rendering renders through that app's own component code, so a drastically different look costs
   CSS/token work only. **Headless Variants are not the divergence mechanism** — they are for a changed
   *field set or arrangement*, or to offer authors a choice within one site. Cheapest first:
   **skin → Headless Variant → new rendering.**
4. **Serialization grain is the COLLECTION, not the site.** A new site in an existing collection gets a
   **site include added to that collection's existing module** — never a second module for the same
   collection, which collides on paths the first module already owns with `CreateUpdateAndDelete`. A new
   *collection* gets a new module, and that module declares a distinct `items.path` or it shares the
   default serialization root and `ser validate` reports the sibling's items as orphans to delete. On a
   shared `cm/`, never run an unscoped `ser` op — always `-i Project.<Brand>`.

## The hard-pin trap — audit before reusing any rendering

"Renderings are collection-scoped, so reuse them" is **not** safe as a blanket statement. Each
rendering's **`Datasource Location`** must be checked first:

- **site-relative** (`query:$site/...`) → resolves per-site, safe for N sites;
- **hard GUID** → resolves to one site's `/Data` forever. Reusing it on a second site points the Pages
  datasource picker at the **first** site's `/Data`, and an author silently writes the new site's
  content into the old site's tree.

**Nothing catches this** — no unit test, no code review, no build gate, no `ser validate`. It is an
authoring-path defect. Chrome (header/footer/nav) is the likely offender: built first, when only one
site existed. Reused renderings also need one `<X> Folder` item per rendering under the new site's
`/Data`, or the Pages picker is empty and reads as a broken component.

## Fork, don't mutate — and the one exception

**Default: do not mutate a shared collection-scoped rendering a live site depends on.** Create new
per-purpose renderings with a site-relative `Datasource Location`, name-prefixed so the shared folder
stays legible, and leave the original defect filed as its own work item in the owning product.

- **Unattended / `fully_autonomous` / any agent-initiated run → fork, no exception.** The failure is
  silent and unrecoverable, and no gate catches it.
- **Remediating the shared rendering in place is permitted only** with an operator present, a captured
  baseline of the live site, and a non-prod rehearsal — and it is then a `go_live`-class decision,
  because it is a real-tenant mutation with blast radius on a live property.

Verify mechanically either way: assert no datasource item for the new site exists anywhere under the
old site's path.
